# Chapter 3 — Life Makes Its Conditions

*The environment of tomorrow includes the consequences of being alive today.*

A living cell couples processes that could not continue in the same way if they occurred separately. An energetically favorable reaction can help support an otherwise unfavorable transformation. A membrane restricts some exchanges while permitting others. Gradients can be maintained and used to drive work. Some of that work produces the components that maintain the organization itself.

The cell gives the creation cycle its clearest example of self-production. Its organization integrates earlier chemical capabilities into a level that can use gradients to renew its own working conditions. The theory defines continuation through recurring organization, requiring autopoiesis to explain that recurrence. This does not mean every cell must produce offspring: renewed operations within an individual and new individuals across a lineage are different occurrences. Interrupt the regenerative relations severely enough and familiar molecules can remain where the living organization once operated.

The cell nevertheless begins with inheritances it did not make. The early universe supplied mostly hydrogen and helium; later stellar processes and events such as neutron-star mergers supplied a wider range of elements. Biological organization uses that material history without turning stars into organisms or suggesting that their activity anticipated life.[1]

When we look at a cell, we see those possibilities reorganized. The elements have become components of membranes, enzymes, and molecules that store and transmit hereditary information. Their chemical properties remain necessary, but a list of them does not explain the organization they sustain. The same elements can exist in a dead cell. Life involves their participation in continuing processes.

Imagine, as a conceptual comparison, a chamber containing all the necessary molecular ingredients in the wrong places and concentrations. The inventory could be impressive. Yet an inventory does not by itself transport a substance across a membrane, couple a favorable reaction to an unfavorable one, repair a damaged component, or reproduce an inherited arrangement. Organization concerns which processes enable which others, and under what conditions their relationships continue.

This does not make organization an additional immaterial substance. It makes relationships among material processes part of what must be explained. A membrane is a physical structure; its selective permeability changes the environment in which reactions occur. Reactions can in turn help produce and maintain the membrane. Neither side alone describes the loop.

Francisco Varela, Humberto Maturana, and Ricardo Uribe used the term autopoiesis to characterize such an organization of self-production. In their formulation, a network of processes produces components that regenerate the network and constitute its unity. Their concern was the maintenance of a living organization, which they distinguished from the separate fact that an organism might reproduce.[2]

That distinction is useful. A living individual does not stop being alive because it is not producing offspring. It must nevertheless maintain conditions under which its own activity can continue. Reproduction extends a lineage; maintenance sustains a particular organized process. In the creation cycle, access to usable energy may improve both, but it must do so through actual production and repair. Thermodynamic work on self-replication and criticism of universal growth-rate interpretations reinforce the need to specify those processes.[3]

A thermostat also participates in a feedback loop, but it does not thereby become an organism. It can regulate a temperature while its sensor, wiring, and power source are manufactured and maintained elsewhere. Feedback is a broader category than biological self-production. Calling a device self-regulating tells us something about its behavior; it does not establish that the device regenerates the organization making that behavior possible.

Even a cell is not independent. It needs suitable exchanges with an environment. Its boundary is a condition of organized dependence, not an escape from dependence. The word “self” can mislead if it conjures a little sovereign entity manufacturing itself out of nothing. Living maintenance is materially open, energetically supplied, and vulnerable to the loss of necessary conditions.

Picture a small reaction network supplied with simple ingredients. One reaction makes a catalyst that helps a second reaction proceed; that reaction makes something needed elsewhere in the network. The useful unit is the set of supporting relationships. Autocatalytic-set theory gives this idea a precise test: can the reactions be supplied from environmental ingredients, and can catalysts available within that food-generated network collectively support them? Chemical organization theory adds a related way to identify collections of components that are closed under their reactions and can maintain their quantities.[4]

These tests distinguish a heap of ingredients from a possible organization, but actual continuation also depends on rates. A network may contain every required connection while replacing a crucial component too slowly. Wash that component out faster than it is made and the organization can fail without any reaction becoming chemically impossible. Conversely, another route may restore its production. Autopoiesis consequently directs us to replacement, loss, and substitution as observable processes. It explains what must keep happening, while assembly explains how those supporting relations first became available.

This gives quantity into quality a specific biological question. A change in supply or replacement rate may allow a network to maintain a component that previously disappeared too quickly. If that component helps renew the processes that produce it, crossing the maintenance threshold can establish a different regime of continuation. The organization and its rates must be supplied by a model or experiment; a diagram containing a cycle does not establish that it can sustain itself.

The cycle now connects an energy gradient, constrained chemical pathways, and the next organization that makes energy extraction possible. Autopoietic operations regenerate its recurring activity. Its effects then transform the conditions of further assembly. The proposed energetic advantage passes through this return: after its added costs, a new route to food or another usable gradient must support the operations that produce the organization again. Reproduction into additional individuals requires its own machinery and inheritance; it is one route to recurring organization, not a substitute for the renewal of each living individual.

The distinction will matter when we reach economic systems. A social extension of autopoiesis must identify recurring operations and the relationships they regenerate, together with the external supplies they require. A surviving building or a balanced feedback loop is insufficient evidence. Nor does self-production establish that the organization maximizes entropy production; that remains a separate part of the book's hypothesis.

This also leaves the origin of life unresolved. Describing how an established cell maintains itself is not a demonstration of how the first such organizations arose. An account of that origin must investigate pathways from earlier chemistry, including intermediate processes that need not have possessed every feature of modern cells. It cannot use the finished cell to smuggle its own starting conditions into existence.

The early appearance of life gives this transition a particular urgency. Mineral evidence indicates interaction with liquid water by about 4.4 billion years ago. The roughly 3.48-billion-year-old Dresser Formation preserves a suite of microbial signatures. Older claims include potentially biological carbon in a 4.1-billion-year-old zircon, whose interpretation remains disputed.[5] These records place constraints on when conditions and organisms existed. They do not provide two adjacent observations of a boiling ocean and the first living system.

Other methods reach further back than the clearer preserved traces. A 2024 molecular-clock study placed the last universal common ancestor of present-day life around 4.2 billion years ago. That ancestor already belongs to an evolutionary history; it is not the first act of abiogenesis. A 2026 thermal model identified a favorable window around 4.33 billion years ago for persistent RNA-related chemistry as impact disruption declined.[6] Taken together, the reconstructions are consistent with a relatively rapid early transition once suitable environments persisted. Their dates remain model-dependent rather than a continuous record of life's beginning.

The book's hypothesis is that assembly need not be astronomically unlikely. Once the relevant conditions and supporting hierarchy exist, a new organization can emerge promptly. Counting every imaginable arrangement of a finished organism gives us the wrong starting problem. The first living organization would have arisen through available chemical transitions, using components and relationships already produced by earlier physical history. It did not have to sample every arrangement that an observer could write down.

Return to the reaction network. Its ingredients cannot combine in arbitrary ways with equal probability. The available reactions, conservation relations, catalysts, and rates determine which sequences can proceed. A product that supports another reaction changes what can happen next. The network therefore inherits constraints as well as ingredients. Autocatalytic-set analysis and chemical organization theory help describe these dependencies; their structural criteria still need a kinetic account.[4] The effective space of reachable configurations can be much narrower than the space of imagined combinations, and its pathways need not be sampled uniformly.

Some of these constraints are Platonic in the book's sense: they concern formal relations realized by the material system. A dependency between reactions is not another molecule floating beside the reactants. A conservation relation cannot be picked out of the solution with tweezers. Such constraints can be invisible to ordinary inspection while their consequences remain measurable. Our failure to see the organizing relation can make an outcome appear more surprising than it is. The proposal is that reality's accessible state space is far more structured and constrained than our inventories of possible combinations suggest. Ignorance of that structure should not be converted into a probability of miraculous success.

The hierarchy of matter matters for the same reason. Earlier processes supply elements, compounds, environments, and persistent structures that later organization can use. Energy must also arrive in a usable form and be coupled to the required transformations. An abundance of heat is insufficient if it destroys the intermediates. Earth could therefore possess abundant energy while lacking a persistent route to living organization. Reduced disruption could make accumulated components usable together while leaving gradients available to drive their renewal. The relevant hierarchy is complete enough for this transition when those supporting relationships can operate together; it is not a claim that material development has reached a final stage.

In this hypothesis, assembly begins as soon as those enabling conditions are in place, on the timescale of the reactions and accumulation needed to realize it. The long wait may belong chiefly to the construction of the conditions. Once they exist, there need be no further astronomical wait for a complete design to be guessed. This is the proposed meaning of finding a solution immediately: physical processes follow newly accessible pathways, without foresight or an exhaustive search. It remains a hypothesis about abiogenesis. A narrow pathway can still be slow or fail, and the geological record cannot establish a zero waiting time. The decisive question is whether independently specified conditions predict prompt formation rather than merely being called ready after life appears.

The timing therefore matters, but it does not isolate the energetic cause. We observe a planet on which life arose early enough for observers to develop. Kipping's Bayesian treatment explicitly addresses this selection effect and finds modest support for a typically rapid origin under its assumptions.[7] The chronology motivates the book's question: did persistent conditions make energy-using, self-renewing chemistry readily accessible? Demonstrating the proposed preference for greater extraction would additionally require comparing pathways, their energetic returns, and their continuation. A short inferred interval cannot supply that comparison by itself.

Hydrothermal ecosystems give the energetic question a physical setting. At the Lost City vent field, Deborah Kelley and colleagues documented seawater reacting with mantle rocks to produce alkaline fluids rich in hydrogen and methane. Microorganisms inhabit the porous carbonate chimneys built where those fluids emerge. Here geological reactions, chemical supply, physical structure, and living activity meet within one submarine system.[8]

Eric Smith and Harold Morowitz give this connection an origin-of-life interpretation. Their proposal is that metabolism emerged as a channel through which geochemical free energy could be released more readily. Energy can be available while reactions that would release it remain slow. A new chemical organization could change that access. Their 2007 paper argues for this possibility while explicitly acknowledging that geochemical driving had not been quantitatively shown to force complex living order into existence.[9]

In their later book, Smith and Morowitz develop the biosphere as a planetary organization emerging through successive transitions. Interfaces between rock and water supply chemical differences, and the proposed route to life passes through organized chemistry before fully developed cells. The starting point is therefore an interacting environment with an energetic history. Modern vent ecosystems help expose those connections, but they are not preserved samples of the first life.[10]

The chemical detail matters to the argument about search. Smith and Morowitz studied the reverse citric-acid cycle as a candidate early metabolic core, asking whether carbon chemistry and energetics could favor its organization over competing pathways. They also proposed that later biological layers could be favored by improving core reaction rates. This supplies a specific version of the book's constrained-assembly hypothesis: investigate which chemical relationships make an organization accessible and persistent, instead of assigning equal chances to arbitrary combinations.[11]

A laboratory result makes one part of the vent proposal more concrete. In 2020, Reuben Hudson and colleagues used a microfluidic apparatus containing an iron–nickel sulfide precipitate between contrasting fluids. With hydrogen, carbon dioxide, and a suitable pH difference, they produced formate, a simple organic compound. Removing hydrogen or sufficiently reducing the pH difference left no detectable product in the reported comparisons. This demonstrates a specified abiotic reaction under constructed conditions; it does not produce a living system.[12]

Calling life the most efficient way to unlock vent chemistry compresses several possible claims. A pathway might release free energy faster, conserve a larger fraction for useful transformations, or sustain more organization after paying its maintenance costs. Chapter 2 distinguishes those measures. Smith and Morowitz's suggestion of an optimal metabolic channel motivates a comparison among alternatives; it does not establish a universal winner. The book's proposal is that a pathway gains a continuing advantage when its energetic return helps regenerate the organization providing access.[9][13]

The creation cycle now has a candidate chemical sequence. Geology supplies a gradient and a constrained reaction environment. The next chemical organization must enable access to that energy. Autopoietic renewal is then required to explain its continuing recurrence. The dialectical interpretation concerns a network whose products become conditions for their own production, whose inherited chemical capabilities are reorganized beyond their earlier limits, and whose production rates cross a threshold relative to losses. These are proposed instances of interpenetration, negation of the negation, and quantity into quality. The cited studies supply components of this argument; they do not demonstrate the complete transition from a vent to recurrent, self-producing life.[13]

This mechanism also sharpens the timing question. The 4.33-billion-year thermal window concerns persistent opportunities for RNA-related chemistry; the vent account concerns a possible organization of energy and reactions. They address different requirements and cannot be combined into a dated observation of life beginning at a deep-sea vent. Their connection is a research question: when could a persistent setting also support the proposed chemical pathway?[6][10][12]

The transition remains to be reconstructed in detail. What can be followed more directly is the consequence of living organizations repeatedly changing their surroundings. The environment becomes an accumulation of their activity, and later organisms inherit conditions partly produced by earlier life. The proposed early opening leads into a history of further openings and obstructions.

This distinction changes what we should look for in a possible trace of life. An intricate object alone is not enough: nonliving processes can make intricate objects. A population of similar elaborate molecules raises a different question. What repeatedly channels construction toward those products?

Assembly theory, developed by Lee Cronin, Sara Walker, and collaborators, offers one way to investigate that question. It relates a construction measure to abundance. The assembly index concerns a shortest sequence of joining operations under specified rules, with previously formed substructures available for reuse.[14] It measures something about a possible construction route, not a complete record of what actually happened.

Consider an artificial example. Begin with a tile marked A and allow two available strings to be joined. With reuse allowed, AA can become AAAA and then AAAAAAAA in three joins. Adding one tile at a time takes longer. Changing the allowed starting pieces or charging separately for every copied intermediate changes the problem. The result depends on the rules, and the short route does not prove that the object was made that way.

Many copies can make organized production a plausible explanation, but the inference must survive comparisons with nonbiological processes, contamination, and selective measurement. Critics have also challenged how much independent information the methods provide relative to other measures.[15] The argument of this chapter does not require assembly theory to settle those disputes. Its useful question is narrower: what retained pathways make repeated construction possible?

Frequency is part of the book's definition of continuing assembly. An isolated complex object and a repeatedly realized organization require different explanations. Here the proposed necessary mechanism is autopoiesis: the system's operations reproduce the organization through which those operations recur. This extends the argument beyond a construction index or a count of copies. Those measures motivate the question, but neither logically supplies the self-production mechanism.[16]

The boundary matters. A storeroom can contain a million manufactured copies while production has stopped. A factory can repeatedly make an object whose own operations never renew anything. For this book's account, identify the larger productive organization and show which of its returns regenerate its capacities; do not simply rename the stock or the repetition autopoiesis. Copy number, production rate, loss rate, and renewal frequency must be recorded separately. Frequent occurrence without the claimed regenerative relationship would challenge the proposed necessity, even where the object is complex.[16]

Nor does a stock reveal a rate. Two samples can contain the same number of molecules while one has rapid production and loss and the other changes very slowly. For a specified boundary, additions and departures determine the change in stock. Lifetime and repair matter alongside copying. A useful bridge may serve a community for generations without multiplying into many bridges.

What living organization contributes to this comparison is a relation among activities: some products constrain processes that renew those products or their supporting organization. Reproduction across generations adds another question, concerning how such organization is transmitted and varied. We should distinguish the two before asking how either can transform an environment.

A higher assembly, in this account, joins earlier capabilities into a new operating organization. Its proposed energetic advantage must be measured at that level. A cell's isolated reaction, the cell maintaining that reaction, and a community exchanging products have different boundaries. Greater extraction by the community cannot simply be assigned to every member. The prediction concerns the extra access enabled by the assembled relationships and whether it sustains their continuation.

Photosynthesis provides a planetary example. In oxygenic photosynthesis, organisms use light in a process that draws electrons from water and releases oxygen. But the presence of oxygen-producing organisms is not equivalent to a modern oxygen-rich atmosphere. Oxygen can react with other substances and be consumed. Accumulation depends on the relation between sources and sinks, not simply on whether production has begun.

The geological problem is therefore historical. Researchers use traces preserved in rocks to reconstruct changing atmospheric and oceanic conditions. Sulfur isotopes are among those traces: their patterns can preserve evidence about atmospheric chemistry under very low oxygen conditions.[17] Work on the timing of the Great Oxidation Event places a major transformation roughly 2.4 billion years ago, while also examining its extended and uneven course.[18] A dated transition is not a claim that every part of the planet changed at once.

This is another stock-and-flow problem, but one with chemical feedbacks. Production alone does not determine the atmospheric stock. The substances available to react with oxygen, their exposure, biological activity, and geological processes all matter. A source can operate for a long time before the balance permits a conspicuous accumulation. Conversely, a change in a sink can alter the atmosphere without requiring an equivalent sudden change in production.

That logic is worth dwelling on because it resists the temptation to make every transformation the result of one breakthrough. A new capability can be necessary and still insufficient. Its consequences depend on the rest of the system. The same process can have small effects in one setting and reorganizing effects in another.

Oxygenation also makes it impossible to treat environmental improvement as a neutral phrase. Oxygen supports forms of metabolism familiar to us. It can also damage organisms unable to tolerate its chemistry. A world becoming more hospitable to one way of living can become less hospitable to another. The environment is not climbing a ladder toward a universally better condition. Its changing composition redistributes possibilities.

This is the cycle generating its own next conditions. Organisms alter chemical conditions, modify surfaces, consume resources, and produce substances used by others. Their descendants encounter a world partly changed by that activity. Some changes create usable resources; others introduce toxic effects or new bottlenecks. Greater extraction can support continued organization while also producing the conditions that later force it to change.

Niche-construction theory makes this feedback explicit. Kevin Laland, John Odling-Smee, and Marcus Feldman examine how organisms modify environments and thereby alter conditions of selection, including through cultural practices.[19] The environment is partly an inheritance of earlier activity. This book draws on that established argument; calling life a maker of its conditions is not a claim to have discovered the feedback. The further comparison with industry and institutions must identify its own forms of inheritance and control.

An environment made by life is not necessarily an environment controlled by life. Consequences can exceed the capacities of the organisms that produce them. Feedback can stabilize some relationships and destabilize others. The fact that an activity has continued for a long time is no guarantee that its accumulated effects will remain compatible with its continuation.

The same care is required when we describe life thermodynamically. An organism can maintain local organization while exchanging matter and energy with its surroundings and contributing to entropy production. There is no contradiction between a cell's continuing order and the second law. The relevant accounting includes the larger system, not merely the selected structure we admire.

The creation-cycle hypothesis predicts that access to greater usable energy can support the regeneration and reproduction of biological assemblies. Variation and inheritance would help turn that advantage into a history of retained capabilities. Whether such development also approaches a constrained maximum of entropy production is a stronger question. The MEPP literature does not already establish that extension to all evolution.[20] We must investigate the local energetic advantage and the proposed extremum separately.

The object being compared matters. A cell can reduce the energy needed for a task and thereby support more descendants or activity within an ecosystem. It can also simply use less energy without a compensating expansion. A population-level claim cannot be inferred from a single-cell measurement, and an ecosystem-level claim needs its own boundary. These distinctions turn the hypothesis into questions that can be tested rather than a demand that every organism burn fuel faster.

A hypothetical organism that spends energy faster than it can replenish its supplies has not secured an evolutionary advantage. One that reduces its maintenance costs may persist where a more demanding competitor cannot. Another may profit from a previously inaccessible resource and expand. These possibilities differ. Their common requirement is a workable relation among access, expenditure, maintenance, and reproduction.

A laboratory experiment allows us to see one such relation with unusual precision. In 1988, Richard Lenski's long-term evolution experiment began with twelve populations of *Escherichia coli*. The growth medium limited glucose but also contained citrate. Under the experiment's oxygenated conditions, the ancestral bacteria could not use that citrate as a carbon source. By approximately 31,500 generations, a citrate-using variant had appeared in one population. The researchers could compare this change with earlier samples preserved from the experiment.[21]

The resource had been present before the new capacity. Its mere presence did not make it available to the ancestral lineage. “Available” had two meanings: detectable in the medium and accessible through the organism's existing machinery. The innovation changed the relation between them.

The archived samples made history experimentally approachable. Researchers could restart populations from earlier points and ask whether the later capacity arose with equal ease from different ancestral states. The results supported a role for historical preconditions in the lineage that acquired citrate use. The opportunity was not equally accessible from every starting point.[21]

Later work investigated the distinction between the initial capacity and its improvement. Quandt and colleagues identified a mutation increasing expression of another transporter, DctA, that substantially improved citrate use. Reconstructing the relevant transporter changes also showed that earlier mutations were not all indispensable to the final, refined capacity. Historical conditions could matter by allowing an initially weak innovation to persist long enough for improvement, rather than by remaining necessary components of its eventual form.[22] A new door can open only partway. Subsequent changes can make passage through it more effective.

That distinction anticipates the first steam engine. A mature capability may look like an obvious advantage while its first accessible version is costly and fragile. The transition requires an organization in which that version can exist. In the bacterium, the relevant support concerns a particular genetic and experimental setting; in the mine, it concerns fuel, construction, and economic relations. The analogy proposes a common problem of crossing an assembly barrier, while the causal details remain different.

This gives us three questions that are often compressed into the word invention. What makes a change accessible? What first establishes the new function? What subsequently makes that function effective enough to transform the population's activity? The answers need not involve the same event.

The experiment does not show that organisms choose useful mutations in anticipation of a resource. It shows that the consequences and accessibility of variation depend on inherited organization. A change that matters in one genetic background may have another effect, or no comparable effect, in another. History changes the neighborhood of possibilities.

Nothing in this lesson overturns evolutionary biology. Mutation, selection, drift, ecological interaction, and historical contingency already belong to its explanatory resources. The assembly perspective earns its place only if it helps us connect these processes more clearly or ask additional testable questions. Renaming a familiar mechanism is not a discovery.

For this book, the experiment's value is its concrete separation of resource from capability. It prevents us from picturing adaptation as an abstract desire for more energy. The bacteria needed a materially realizable route. Once a route existed, its population consequences still depended on costs, competition, and the supplied environment. The experimental system was maintained by people; its regular transfers and medium were not achievements of the bacteria themselves.

That boundary matters. If we described the expanding population as entirely self-sustaining while omitting the laboratory, we would mistake a supported process for an autonomous world. The same error appears when a factory is said to produce itself while its workers, power, and suppliers vanish from the account. The relevant boundary depends on the question, but the excluded dependencies cannot be wished away.

Nor should one innovation stand in for all evolution. Some successful changes open resources. Others improve repair, avoid hazards, reduce costs, alter interactions, or preserve a workable arrangement under changing conditions. Some lineages lose functions when those functions are costly and unnecessary in their environment. Complexity is not an obligation imposed on every survivor.

A useful comparative program would therefore look for the conditions under which new access matters most. Was the resource genuinely limiting? Could another necessary input become the next constraint? Did the innovation increase descendants, persistence, or both? Did competitors gain access to its byproducts? Did the altered population change the environment in ways that later undermined its advantage?

These questions make the energy argument stronger by giving it possible failures. If the newly accessible resource makes no difference because another requirement is absent, the proposed chain stops at a particular link. If the change spreads because it reduces a cost rather than increasing throughput, we should say so. A framework capable of recognizing only expansions will misread the many ways organisms continue.

The most interesting histories involve a combination. Maintenance conserves a workable organization while variation changes some of its capacities. A new capacity alters the setting in which further variations occur. The lineage neither begins again from nothing nor simply executes an unchanging program. It inherits an organization that both enables and restricts its next steps.

This is a material form of the dialectical relation introduced in chapter 1. A boundary permits a distinctive internal chemistry and limits exchange. A stable metabolism keeps an organism alive and constrains which changes can be tolerated. A successful population modifies its environment, which can then select against some of the arrangements that previously succeeded. The oppositions matter because identifiable processes connect them.

We should be wary, however, of making the planet itself an organism merely because these loops exist. Earth contains interacting biological and geological processes. Some interactions can contribute to stability over a range of conditions. That does not establish a single planetary interest, reproductive unit, or purpose. A system can have feedback without having an intention, and an environment can sustain life without optimizing conditions for every living thing.

The world that life made is therefore neither a prepared home nor a benevolent manager. It is an inheritance full of opportunities and hazards. Its atmosphere contains the consequences of earlier activity. Its living populations contain histories of variation and survival. Its present possibilities depend on both.

The materialist commitment becomes specific here. An explanation of a living capability must connect it to realizable organization, energy pathways, environmental conditions, and a history that can be investigated. The commitment does not mean that every present organism can already be exhaustively explained. It means that gaps in our account are invitations to study these relationships, rather than evidence that organization floats free of matter.

It also changes the image of a creature acting in the world. The creature is a continuation of processes older than itself, but it is not therefore causally idle. Its activity can transform conditions inherited by others. Dependence and efficacy coexist. To have been made by a world does not prevent something from helping remake that world.

Humans participate in this history through biological inheritance. We also preserve and transmit practices through social learning. A person can acquire a way of preparing food, repairing a tool, or organizing a harvest without inheriting the practice in a DNA sequence. The learning still requires bodies, materials, and time. Its route of transmission changes what can accumulate and how quickly an inherited capacity can spread.

That second inheritance does not abolish the first. It adds another set of relationships through which the environment of tomorrow is produced. A field contains selected plants and worked soil; it also contains the consequences of remembered seasons, shared skills, obligations, and decisions about who will eat the harvest.

The next chapter begins with familiar discoveries in physics: relativity and the reconsideration of wave and particle descriptions. It asks why related answers become accessible to different investigators, and why finding them can require changing the categories of the search. We will then work backward to the steam engine, where support for a new assembly is more tangible, before asking whether the same principles could have built the universe itself.

